course: biology
lesson: 238
title: Ants: colonies, queens, farmers and living bridges
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: One ant on its own is not very clever, but a colony of thousands works like one big animal that finds food, builds, farms and defends itself.
explain: Drop a crumb on the kitchen floor and soon a line of ants is marching to it: the first ant left a smell trail and the others follow it.
explain: In the nest a queen lays the eggs and her daughters, the workers, do all the other jobs; no ant gives orders, each one follows simple rules.
explain: Together ants farm fungus, keep aphids like cows, build bridges out of their own bodies and float through floods on living rafts.
fact: Ants leave smell trails called pheromones; when a trail leads to good food, more ants follow and lay more smell, so the trail gets stronger.
fact: In a nest the queen lays the eggs, and the workers, all females and sisters, care for the young, dig and fetch food.
fact: Some ant queens can live for nearly 30 years, the longest-lived insects known.
fact: On «flying ant day» young queens and males fly out to mate, and each new queen pulls off her wings and starts a new nest.
fact: Leafcutter ants do not eat leaves; they chew them to grow a fungus garden and eat the fungus, and they farmed long before people did.
fact: Some ants guard aphids from ladybirds and stroke them for sweet honeydew, and some keep aphid eggs in the nest over winter.
fact: Army ants link their bodies into bridges and even a living nest called a bivouac, with no ant in charge.
fact: In floods fire ants link legs into a floating raft with the queen and young in the middle, and the raft can float for weeks.
fact: Ants can carry many times their own weight, because small animals are strong for their size; a dog-sized ant could hardly stand.
fact: The bullet ant of South America has one of the most painful stings of any insect.
fact: Ants carry dead nestmates to a rubbish pile, which keeps germs away from the nest, recognising death by a smell; a live ant dabbed with that smell gets carried out kicking.
fact: Termites are sometimes called white ants but are not ants at all; they are closer to cockroaches, and their colonies have a king as well as a queen.
fact: Some honeypot ant workers swell with sweet liquid and hang from the nest roof like living jars, storing food for hard times.
exercise: How do ants find the way to a crumb? | They follow a smell trail left by other ants | They see it from far away with their big eyes | They hear it fall | The queen tells each one
exercise: Why does a busy ant trail get stronger? | More ants follow it and lay more smell | The ants paint it with mud | The wind blows the smell stronger and stronger | It does not change at all
exercise: What happens if you wipe part of an ant trail with a wet cloth? | The ants get confused and wander until they find it again | The ants march straight on | The ants all turn round, go home for ever and never come out again | The ants grow wings
exercise: Who lays the eggs in an ant nest? | The queen | The workers | The male ants | The oldest worker
exercise: Who are the worker ants? | Females, all sisters, daughters of the queen | Males, all of them the sons of the king ant | Ants from many other nests | Babies that never grow up
exercise: What happens on flying ant day? | Young queens and males fly out to mate and start new nests | All the ants leave to find a warmer country for the winter | Old workers fly away to die | The nest moves to a new tree
exercise: What does a new queen do with her wings? | Pulls them off and digs a new nest | Keeps flying for years | Gives them to her workers to use | Grows bigger ones
exercise: What do leafcutter ants eat? | Fungus grown on chewed-up leaves | The leaves themselves, chewed up | Other ants | Sand
exercise: Why do some ants look after aphids? | To get sweet honeydew, protecting them from ladybirds | To eat them all at once | To turn them into new queens for the nest when the old queen dies | To use them as pillows
exercise: How do army ants cross a gap? | They hold onto each other to make a living bridge | They jump across one by one | They wait for the gap to close | The queen carries them over on her back, a few at a time
exercise: Who is the boss of an army ant bridge? | No ant; each follows simple rules | The queen, giving orders from the nest | The biggest soldier ant in the colony | The ant at the very front of the bridge
exercise: What do fire ants do in a flood? | Link legs into a floating raft | Climb onto each other's backs and drown | Swim like fish | Dig deeper until it ends
exercise: Why can ants carry many times their own weight? | Small animals are strong for their size | Ants have legs made of steel | Ants are filled with air | Ants carry heavy things with their wings
exercise: What is the bullet ant famous for? | One of the most painful stings of any insect | Being the fastest ant | Being the biggest ant that ever lived on Earth | Eating metal
exercise: How do ants know a nestmate is dead? | By a chemical smell it gives off | It stops singing its little ant song | It turns white all over, like a ghost | The queen tells them all about it
exercise: What are «white ants» really? | Termites, which are not ants at all | Baby ants that have not turned brown yet | Ants covered in flour | Ant queens
exercise: Why do honeypot ants hang from the nest roof? | They store sweet liquid for hard times | They are fast asleep for the whole of winter | They are ill and too weak to walk about | They are guarding the roof against any enemies
test: A cartoon ant lifts a whole sandwich. Which is true? | Ants can carry many times their weight, though not a whole sandwich alone | Ants are too weak to lift a crumb | Ants are as strong as elephants | Only cartoon ants can carry things; real ants can only drag things along the ground
test: «they made a giant ant in a film, it must be super strong» What would really happen? | Its legs could hardly hold its weight | It could lift a bus | It would fly | It would be faster than a car
test: «wiped the ant line with a wet cloth and now they're running round like idiots» Why? | The smell trail is gone, so they search until they find it | They are angry at the cloth | They are drowning | They forgot what ants are and where they are meant to be going
test: A friend lifts a paving stone and sees white eggs and grubs. Which is true? | Workers care for the queen's young there | The grubs are a different kind of insect | The eggs belong to the male ants | The grubs are dead ants
test: What would happen if leafcutter ants lost their fungus garden? | They would lose their food | Nothing, they eat the leaves anyway | They would grow wings and fly off | They would farm aphids instead at once
test: «saw ants dragging a dead ant out, is that a funeral» What is going on? | They take dead nestmates to a rubbish pile to keep germs away | They are burying it with flowers | They are taking it to a doctor | They want to eat it in the nest, where it is safe from birds
test: A ladybird lands on a rose full of aphids. What might the ants on the rose do? | Chase it off to protect their aphids | Help it eat the aphids | Ignore it | Carry it into their nest as a guest
test: Count them: 5 ants walk in a line. How many legs? | 30 legs | 20 legs | 40 legs | 25 legs
test: Count them: 3 ants and 1 spider on the windowsill. How many legs? | 26 legs | 20 legs | 24 legs | 32 legs
test: A termite nest and an ant nest. Which has a king? | The termite nest | The ant nest, always | Both of them have one | Neither of them has one
test: «ant queens just live a summer like flies» What is wrong? | Some queens can live nearly 30 years | Right, they all die in a week | Ant queens never die | Queens live for exactly one year and then die
exam: Identify the means by which worker ants guide their nestmates to a source of food. | Trails of chemical scent designated pheromones | Visual signals emitted by the queen | Vocal calls of high frequency | Maps scratched into the soil
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet of leafcutter ants? | They cultivate a fungus upon chewed leaves and consume the fungus | They consume the leaves directly | They consume soil exclusively | They subsist on the nectar of flowers gathered during the hours of daylight
exam: Ascertain the classification of the insects colloquially designated «white ants». | They are termites, more nearly related to cockroaches than to ants | They are juvenile ants | They are a species of albino ant | They are the queens of ant colonies
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of the worker caste of an ant colony. | The workers are exclusively female and are the daughters of the queen | The workers are exclusively male | The workers are the sons of the king, who rules the colony together with the queen | The workers are of both sexes in equal number
